## 4. Certificates & Secrets

### 4.1 cert-manager Operator for Red Hat OpenShift

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Automates issuance and renewal of TLS certificates from ACME (Let's Encrypt — connected only), Venafi, Vault PKI, or an internal CA. Issues `Certificate` objects into secrets consumed by Routes/Ingress and workloads. Removes manual certificate rotation for app-level TLS. |
| **Package** | `openshift-cert-manager-operator` |
| **Namespace / scope** | `cert-manager-operator` (operator), `cert-manager` (controllers) / single namespace |
| **Channel** | `stable-v1` |
| **Primary CRs** | `ClusterIssuer`, `Issuer`, `Certificate` |

```yaml
# Internal CA issuer from an enterprise intermediate CA (disconnected-friendly)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata: {name: corp-intermediate-ca, namespace: cert-manager}
type: kubernetes.io/tls
data: {tls.crt: <base64 intermediate cert>, tls.key: <base64 key>}
---
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata: {name: corp-ca}
spec:
  ca: {secretName: corp-intermediate-ca}
---
# Vault PKI alternative
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata: {name: vault-pki}
spec:
  vault:
    server: https://vault.example.com:8200
    path: pki_int/sign/ocp-prod
    caBundle: <base64 vault CA>
    auth:
      kubernetes:
        role: cert-manager
        mountPath: /v1/auth/kubernetes
        serviceAccountRef: {name: cert-manager}
---
# Example certificate
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata: {name: api-gateway-tls, namespace: app-team-a}
spec:
  secretName: api-gateway-tls
  duration: 2160h
  renewBefore: 360h
  dnsNames: [api-gateway.apps.ocp-prod.example.com]
  issuerRef: {name: corp-ca, kind: ClusterIssuer}
```
Production notes: pair with the **cert-manager Operator's** `CertManager` CR to pin controller resources and enable `--enable-certificate-owner-ref`; use it also to rotate the wildcard ingress certificate automatically (`Certificate` in `openshift-ingress` + patch IngressController).

### 4.2 External Secrets Operator (ESO) / Vault CSI

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Syncs secrets from an external vault (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, CyberArk) into Kubernetes `Secret` objects on a schedule, so secrets are never committed to Git and are rotated centrally. **Vault CSI / Secrets Store CSI** mounts secrets as files at pod start instead of creating Secret objects. |
| **Packages** | `external-secrets-operator` (Red Hat build — `redhat-operators`, GA in 4.22; or community `external-secrets-operator` from certified/community catalog); `secrets-store-csi-driver-operator` for CSI |
| **Namespace / scope** | `external-secrets-operator` / all namespaces |
| **Channel** | `stable-v1` / `tech-preview-v0.1` (verify) |
| **Primary CRs** | `ExternalSecretsConfig` (Red Hat operand), `ClusterSecretStore`, `SecretStore`, `ExternalSecret` |

```yaml
# Red Hat build: deploy the operand
apiVersion: operator.openshift.io/v1alpha1
kind: ExternalSecretsConfig
metadata: {name: cluster}
spec: {}
---
# Vault backend with Kubernetes auth
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata: {name: vault-prod}
spec:
  provider:
    vault:
      server: https://vault.example.com:8200
      path: kv
      version: v2
      caBundle: <base64 vault CA>
      auth:
        kubernetes:
          mountPath: kubernetes
          role: ocp-prod-eso
          serviceAccountRef: {name: eso-vault-auth, namespace: external-secrets}
---
# Sync a secret into an app namespace
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ExternalSecret
metadata: {name: db-credentials, namespace: app-team-a}
spec:
  refreshInterval: 1h
  secretStoreRef: {name: vault-prod, kind: ClusterSecretStore}
  target: {name: db-credentials, creationPolicy: Owner}
  data:
    - secretKey: username
      remoteRef: {key: app-team-a/db, property: username}
    - secretKey: password
      remoteRef: {key: app-team-a/db, property: password}
```
Production notes: restrict which namespaces may use the `ClusterSecretStore` via `conditions.namespaceSelector`; use one Vault role per namespace/team; combine with etcd encryption (secrets still land in etcd).

