## 6. Networking

### 6.1 MetalLB

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Provides `Service type=LoadBalancer` on bare metal by announcing service IPs via Layer 2 (ARP/NDP) or BGP. Needed for non-HTTP workloads (databases, Kafka, gRPC) that cannot use the OpenShift router. |
| **Package** | `metallb-operator` |
| **Namespace / scope** | `metallb-system` / single namespace |
| **Channel** | `stable` |
| **Primary CRs** | `MetalLB`, `IPAddressPool`, `L2Advertisement`, `BGPPeer`, `BGPAdvertisement` |

```yaml
apiVersion: metallb.io/v1beta1
kind: MetalLB
metadata: {name: metallb, namespace: metallb-system}
spec:
  nodeSelector: {node-role.kubernetes.io/worker: ""}    # speakers on workers only
  logLevel: info
---
apiVersion: metallb.io/v1beta1
kind: IPAddressPool
metadata: {name: prod-l2, namespace: metallb-system}
spec:
  addresses: [192.168.100.200-192.168.100.230]
  autoAssign: false                                      # require explicit annotation
  avoidBuggyIPs: true
---
apiVersion: metallb.io/v1beta1
kind: L2Advertisement
metadata: {name: prod-l2, namespace: metallb-system}
spec:
  ipAddressPools: [prod-l2]
  nodeSelectors: [{matchLabels: {node-role.kubernetes.io/worker: ""}}]
---
# BGP variant (preferred for multi-rack / ECMP)
apiVersion: metallb.io/v1beta2
kind: BGPPeer
metadata: {name: tor-a, namespace: metallb-system}
spec: {myASN: 64512, peerASN: 64500, peerAddress: 192.168.100.1, holdTime: 90s, keepaliveTime: 30s, bfdProfile: default}
```
Request an IP: `metallb.universe.tf/address-pool: prod-l2` annotation on the Service.
Production notes: coordinate the pool with the network team (excluded from DHCP); use BGP with BFD where the switches support it — L2 fails over slower and pins all traffic to one node.

### 6.2 Kubernetes NMState Operator

| | |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Declaratively manages host networking on RHCOS nodes after installation — bonds, VLANs, bridges, additional interfaces, routes, DNS — via `NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy`. Required to create bridges for OpenShift Virtualization, VLAN interfaces for Multus/SR-IOV, or secondary storage networks. |
| **Package** | `kubernetes-nmstate-operator` |
| **Namespace / scope** | `openshift-nmstate` / single namespace |
| **Channel** | `stable` |
| **Primary CRs** | `NMState` (singleton), `NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy`, `NodeNetworkState` (read-only) |

```yaml
apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1
kind: NMState
metadata: {name: nmstate}
---
# Example: VLAN 300 + bridge on the worker bond for storage/VM traffic
apiVersion: nmstate.io/v1
kind: NodeNetworkConfigurationPolicy
metadata: {name: br-storage-vlan300}
spec:
  nodeSelector: {node-role.kubernetes.io/worker: ""}
  maxUnavailable: 1
  desiredState:
    interfaces:
      - name: bond0.300
        type: vlan
        state: up
        vlan: {base-iface: bond0, id: 300}
      - name: br-storage
        type: linux-bridge
        state: up
        ipv4: {enabled: false}
        bridge:
          options: {stp: {enabled: false}}
          port: [{name: bond0.300}]
```
Verify: `oc get nnce` (enactments), `oc get nns <node> -o yaml`.
Production notes: always set `maxUnavailable`; test on one node with a tight `nodeSelector` first; a bad policy can isolate a node — NMState rolls back if the API server becomes unreachable, but not for data-plane-only breakage.

### 6.3 SR-IOV Network Operator & Multus (advanced / telco)

| | |
|---|---|
| **Multus** | Built into OCP (Cluster Network Operator). Lets pods attach **additional** network interfaces via `NetworkAttachmentDefinition` (macvlan, ipvlan, bridge, host-device, SR-IOV). No install needed — just create NADs. |
| **SR-IOV Network Operator** | Package `sriov-network-operator`, ns `openshift-sriov-network-operator`, single ns, channel `stable`. Configures SR-IOV Virtual Functions on supported NICs (Intel E810/X710, Mellanox ConnectX) and exposes them as device-plugin resources for DPDK/high-throughput/low-latency pods. Only for NFV, HPC, or line-rate workloads. |

```yaml
# Multus macvlan attachment (no operator)
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata: {name: storage-net, namespace: app-team-a}
spec:
  config: '{"cniVersion":"0.3.1","type":"macvlan","master":"bond0.300","mode":"bridge",
            "ipam":{"type":"whereabouts","range":"10.30.0.0/24","range_start":"10.30.0.50","range_end":"10.30.0.200"}}'
---
# SR-IOV
apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1
kind: SriovNetworkNodePolicy
metadata: {name: e810-vfs, namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator}
spec:
  resourceName: intel_e810
  nodeSelector: {feature.node.kubernetes.io/network-sriov.capable: "true"}
  numVfs: 16
  nicSelector: {vendor: "8086", deviceID: "1593", pfNames: ["ens2f0"]}
  deviceType: vfio-pci              # or netdevice
  isRdma: false
---
apiVersion: sriovnetwork.openshift.io/v1
kind: SriovNetwork
metadata: {name: sriov-dpdk, namespace: openshift-sriov-network-operator}
spec:
  resourceName: intel_e810
  networkNamespace: telco-app
  vlan: 400
  ipam: '{"type":"static"}'
```
Pods request `resources.limits: {openshift.io/intel_e810: 1}` and annotate `k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: sriov-dpdk`.
Production notes: SR-IOV node policy application reboots nodes; pair with NFD labels and the Performance Profile (Section 11).

